<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karl August von Hardenberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1750">1750</a>–<a href="/years/1822">1822</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Prince Hardenberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karl August von Hardenberg was a Prussian statesman who served as state chancellor from 1810 until his death in 1822. Continuing the reforms begun by Stein after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>'s defeat in 1806, he introduced freedom of trade, emancipated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in 1812 and reformed taxation and the peasants' obligations. He represented Prussia at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>, where it gained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-august-von-hardenberg.jpg" alt="Karl August von Hardenberg" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Lawrence · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Thomas_Lawrence_(1769-1830)_-_Charles_Augustus,_Prince_Hardenberg_(1750-1822)_-_RCIN_404941_-_Royal_Collection.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
